WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.760
The search for alien life
in the Universe

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:06.360
is one of the most intriguing quests
of our time.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.520
From UFOs and little green aliens

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.520
to the possibility of life in the
underground aquifers of Mars,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.320
there's no end to speculation as to
what form this life could take

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.960
and how it could have developed.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.240
There's even a raft of videos
on the internet

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.520
speculating that aliens
must have visited

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.880
ancient civilisations on Earth,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.600
because otherwise, how else
could the Mayans have built

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.600
their incredible temples.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.320
Spoiler - there is no evidence
at all for this.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.680
Frankly, if I was
a Mayan stoneworker from 250AD,

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.080
I would find it deeply insulting.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.000
But a more interesting theory,
and one which has more credibility,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.600
is that of panspermia.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.080
That life exists throughout
the Universe

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.880
and can be transported through space
from one location to another.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.000
Although it's certainly not proven,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.560
a team of prominent scientists
from MIT and Harvard

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.880
have been working on a theory
that some form of life

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.400
was actually delivered to Mars
in this way.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.440
Here's what we know.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.120
Life is incredibly adaptable, just
look at the way our own species

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.400
has managed to thrive
across the globe,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.400
and microorganisms,
such as archaea and bacteria,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.320
over the course of millions
of years of evolution

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.920
have been able to modify themselves

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.360
in order to adapt
to a vast range of conditions.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.600
This means that today there
are microbes that can survive

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:38.360
on a variety of diets - sulphur,
ammonia, the metal manganese,

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.640
and in the presence
or absence of oxygen.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.680
Some even survive in the most extreme
conditions Earth has to offer.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.520
Pyrococcus furiosus
(don't you love that name?)

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.440
thrives in hydrothermal vents
on the seafloor.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.400
It's optimal growing temperature
is 100 degrees Celsius.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.800
Whereas the Antarctic
psychrobacter frigidicola

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.960
prefers things decidedly chillier.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.760
You can also find these extremophiles
hanging out in hot acid,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.200
or surviving desiccation
in salt-crusted deserts.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.800
And some of these microbes can even
deal with several extremes at once.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.160
Deinococcus radiodurans
can be found in both hot springs

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.320
and Antarctic soil,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.200
survive desiccation, and is one
of the most radiation-resistant

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.800
organisms we know.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.680
So these extremophiles are probably
the most oven-ready organisms

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.840
to survive and, potentially, colonise
the hostile environments

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.520
of other planets and moons,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.000
as long as there's liquid water
at least some of the time.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.640
How would these tiny microbes

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.040
even make the journey
to these other places?

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.720
Well, the easiest way
is to hitch a ride with us

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.320
as we explore our
solar system and beyond.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.960
Tersicoccus phoenicis
is a becterium 

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.800
discovered in Nasa spacecraft
cleaning facilities.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.760
Could we have already accidentally
introduced bacteria from Earth

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.080
to the Moon and Mars?

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.200
Another possible way

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.600
for these microbes
to get around the solar system

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.160
is by hitchhiking on meteoroids.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.920
When these crash on a planet,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.040
rocks and debris are shot into space,
generating more meteoroids.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.000
So far, 313 Martian meteorites
have been found on Earth,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.640
and an Earth rock
was also found on the Moon,

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.240
therefore we know there has been
interplanetary transfer of rocks.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.920
Once in space,
the cold and lack of oxygen

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.480
are easily dealt with

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.200
by our hardy voyagers,

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.520
and even regular bacteria
can enter a state of dormancy

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.160
under extreme conditions,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.120
creating thick-walled internal safe
rooms for their DNA called spores.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.240
Heat, cold, acid, drought
and UV-resistant packets

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.760
of bacterial DNA
voyaging through space.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:58.360
Another big problem is that space
is brimming with ionising radiation

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.480
that rips DNA to shreds.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.160
Not a problem for deinococcus.
Clumps of this little fellow

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.880
have survived three years of exposure
to outer space.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.400
Others have survived up to six years
in spore form.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.720
Yet another problem is time.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.200
Space is big,

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.120
so travelling anywhere
takes a long time.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.520
That said, in 2020, Japanese
scientists revived bacteria

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.520
that had laid dormant
at the bottom of the ocean

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.680
for 100 million years.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.160
So perhaps the extraordinary
distances aren't such a problem

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.200
for these tiny space travellers.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.080
The final step is surviving the crash
landing onto your new home.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.880
And bacteria have been shown
to be able to do just that...

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.880
As long as they are in deep fractures
in the cosmic rock.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.120
So it's entirely possible
that microbial life

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.920
has already travelled
to somewhere like Mars.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.960
Conditions there were remarkably
similar to Earth

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.520
3.8 billion years ago.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.720
Could these extremophile microbes

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.920
have already colonised the
underground aquifers of Mars?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.800
If they're already there,

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.000
have they adapted
to their new environment?

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.200
Or maybe life on Earth actually
originated on Mars

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.040
then travelled over to our planet?

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.240
They may not be little green aliens,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.480
or intelligent life
as we understand it,

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.560
but the very possibility that life
could have transferred

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.280
across the solar system and beyond
is deeply intriguing.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.240
And as the James T Webb telescope
begins its search

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.400
for tell-tale signs
of distant life on other planets,

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:35.160
could we perhaps find out that life
is far more inevitable

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.160
than we once thought?

